    "type": "message",
    "user": "U08EXEBDXQD",
    "text": "Also, I have an issue with the `too_many_buffers` and `too_many_contexts` tests. The first one opens two contexts, and for each one of them opens some buffers. Now, for one of them, it does NOT close the buffer descriptor. So, when the test finishes, it does not fail, but then there is still an ongoing closing of the buffer and then the last context. The issue is, that the second test opens `MAX_CONTEXTS` number of contexts. Now, I noticed with my solution that many times, before the last context in the first test gets closed, the second test fails because it cant allocate the maximum number of contexts. On the other hand, when I add closing of the buffer descriptor inside the first test, everything works. So I'm wondering whether this is something I should somehow support? At the end of the day, file descriptors should be closed by the program that uses them, and while I don't leak resources when its not closed, because of that sometimes closing can happen too late.",
